Could someone talk about Crawl? Sure! :P

In Crawl, there are about as many skills as in NetHack, but they are
broken up into four categories: 

 1. Fighting skills, mostly based on weapon class, though also 
    including "throwing," "unarmed combat," and just "fighting"
    which boosts melee generically as well as max HP;

 2. Spell skills, mostly based on spell school, though also
    including "Spellcasting," which boosts magic generically
    as well as max MP and spell slots (you can memorize a finite 
    number of spells);

 3. Armor skills, "Armor," "Shields," and "Dodging," which increase
    the value to you of those three objects or acts, respectively;

 4. Other skills, like "Stealth" or "Traps and Doors" or "Invocations,"
    which make you better at the thing in question. 

Each skill can be at some level from 0 to 27. When you gain XP (usually
by killing a monster, but sometimes from something else), it goes into
your XP pool, and XP from you pool is depleted into skills as you use
them.  So if you often hit things with axes and they hit you while you
are wearing armor, your Axes and Armor skills will go up faster than
anything else. You have some choice about what skills are trained ---
you can turn an individual skill off, meaning it will only train around
25% of the time it might have otherwise --- but that's about it. [1]

Because of this, what you do in the early game and what items you find
in the first few levels have a lot of influence on your skillset. If you
find a powerful spear on the ground and were planning on going axes,
you can't just use the spear for a long time, not #enhance the skill,
get Cleaver, and then switch; you will have invested a lot into being 
good with the spear and thus Polearms. 

Also, the spell school system is much more restrictive. In NetHack,
a wizard can get to the point of casting every spell without too much
trouble. In Crawl, you cannot; you need a high skill in a spell's school
to have a chance to cast it, if it's a high-level spell, even if you
are otherwise maxed out. In NetHack terms, few if any characters would
be able to cast Finger of Death, Polymorph, and Magic Mapping, at 30%
failure rate, let alone 0%.

Add in the Crawl resistance model (in brief: No character will be able
to resist everything all the time) and winning characters mostly look very
different from each other. 

